{{also|crevasse}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|crevice}}, from {{der|en|fro|crevace}}, from {{m|fro|crever||to break, burst}}, from {{der|en|la|crepare||to break, burst, crack}}. {{doublet|en|crevasse}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈkɹɛvɪs/}}
** {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-crevice.wav |Audio (UK)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[narrow]] [[crack]] or [[fissure]], as in a [[rock]] or [[wall]].
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Mariana}}
#*: The mouse, / Behind the mouldering wainscot, shrieked, / Or from the '''crevice''' peer'd about.
#* '''16 March, 1926''', {{w|Virginia Woolf}}, ''letter to V. Sackville-West''
#*: I can't tell you how urbane and sprightly the old poll parrot was; and {{...}} not a pocket, not a '''crevice''', of pomp, humbug, respectability in him: he was fresh as a daisy.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1973|author={{w|Thomas Pynchon}}| title={{w|Gravity's Rainbow}}| passage= A dark turd appears out the '''crevice''', out of the absolute darkness between her white buttocks.}}

